 third moon**, and his herald was going to ride tomorrow morning to the wrong week. Lord Ormund had taken the blame onto his own house. I corrected the moon with my grandfather. In the same letter I added Ollo's word: the line is to be paraphed **as it stands, with the empty box paraphed as empty**. Then I told my lord the fault was mine.\n- **Ollo's name.** I had told Ollo that my lord gave me his name. It was false: I read it in the note on the copy, and I was the one who gave it to my lord. Ollo was starting to suspect my lord because of my sentence. I withdrew it to his face and answered his question truthfully: my lord asked me about the hull, not about the clerk.\n\nI had also told my grandfather his four rules were copied into my volume. They weren't, so I copied them in before he could read my letter. From now on I write the day, the moon and the year every time, and I check in my own letters where a fact came from before I repeat it.\n\n**What I'm waiting for:**\n- the herald's copy of the Port-Réal line, by the 15th;\n- the Villevieille congé, pulled at first hour tomorrow;\n- mestre Norren, who is still silent. If he hasn't answered by the 15th, I tell my lord at table.\n\n**Tomorrow:** the bell's criée goes out on the squares and at the septs. The words are already written. Then three nights of counting the watchmen, noting which night each time. I report the worst trial, not the average, and what the town understands as an observation, not a number.", "ttft_ms": 21913, "type": "result", "duration_ms": 577713, "uuid": "f80d3704-61b1-4aa2-b9ca-b7c313b262da", "ttft_stream_ms": 2883, "time_to_request_ms": 358, "first_content_frame_ms": 3230, "queued_turn_count": 0, "provider": "claude", "logical_session_id": "d17124ff-6984-5c70-ae72-937003c8873b", "model": "defaut"}
